# Pokemon Stadium 2 — fair-heavy (realistic ~7% edits, not adversarial)
# 64 MB N64 ROM. Sits one notch heavier than dm4y (4 MB, real
# translation): meaningfully heavy work for BPS to do, but with edit
# shapes a real ROM hack might make (block swaps, region copies,
# structured tables, masked transforms).

base:   test/data/stadium2/base.z64
sha1:   f3b9f582c97d644c023622973ca04ef3b5a87dfc
desc:   stadium2 fair-heavy (64 MB N64, ~7% changed, realistic shapes)

BPS      | test/data/stadium2/fair-heavy/patch.bps         | synthetic | slap
IPS32    | test/data/stadium2/fair-heavy/patch.ips32       | synthetic | slap
UPS      | test/data/stadium2/fair-heavy/patch.ups         | synthetic | slap
PPF3     | test/data/stadium2/fair-heavy/patch.ppf         | synthetic | slap
NINJA2   | test/data/stadium2/fair-heavy/patch.rup         | synthetic | slap
APS-N64  | test/data/stadium2/fair-heavy/patch.aps         | synthetic | slap
GDIFF    | test/data/stadium2/fair-heavy/patch.gdiff       | synthetic | slap

# slap made each of these. For each format we also made a patch with
# the listed tool, and applied them with both tools — the resulting
# SHAs all agreed.
#
#   IPS32   sips (create), Atmosphère (apply)*
#   UPS     Go ups
#   PPF3    MakePPF3
#   NINJA2  RomPatcher.js
#   APS-N64 RomPatcher.js
#   GDIFF   javaxdelta
#
# * We used their apply algorithm, not the program directly
